      <description>&lt;div class="wi_fic_desc" property="description"&gt;&lt;p&gt;What if, in the trans-fiction trope of the egg meeting their adult self, the adult needed just as much help as the egg did?&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;What if transitioning didn’t solve everything?&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;August Hagemann is in Wisconsin in 2004, and struggling to find safety or comfort in his family or friends during his senior year of high school.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Autumn Hagemann is in California in 2024, and failing to find internal peace despite accepting that they are trans and starting estrogen.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;And every night, both of them dream of a cavernous, frozen cave - a space where they both get chances to talk to the only person who can figure out what is wrong: themselves.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;**************&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The Girl Beneath the Ice, the second novel by Emoria Weidner (author of Aftertaste: A Toxic Yuri) will post chapters to Scribble Hub one month after they have been live on Patreon.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;To read chapters a month early, and support future work, visit Patreon.com/emoriaweidner&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;************&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Content Warnings: The Girl Beneath the Ice is not a hopeless novel, but it deals with serious mental health issues. Depression, dysphoria, and suicidal ideation are present in every chapter. &lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Internalized transphobia and homophobia are also pervasive, as are homophobic/transphobic slurs and self-loathing diatribes.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;There are brief depictions of self harm.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Please use caution while reading and take breaks if necessary.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;*************&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;If you're trans, you have to live.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
